                <pb facs="1715" n="1675" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg1675"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">684A.86/10–854</note>
                <head>No. 909<lb/>The Secretary of State to the Israeli
                    Ambassador (Eban)</head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1954-10-18"
                        >October 18, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">PERSONAL AND Confidential</p>
                <p>Dear Mr. Ambassador: Your letter of October 8, 1954, reached me just prior to my
                    departure from Washington. I returned this morning but, as I told you when we
                    saw each other on October 8, I must leave for Europe tomorrow afternoon where I
                    shall remain until October 24.</p>
                <p>I wanted you to know, however, that I have read your letter very carefully and
                    appreciate its frankness. I am keeping this matter under my personal review and
                    look forward to our continuing our talks on Israel’s problems. However, as
                    certain of the issues involved have found their way into the press and have
                    become the subject of speculation in political circles in this country on the
                    eve of an election, it is probably best that substantive discussions on these
                    issues be postponed until the general atmosphere is calmer.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        John Foster Dulles
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
